.TH BUSYBOX 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
busybox \- one program providing the shell and many Unix commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I applet
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B busybox
.RI [ applet
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B busybox
.BR \-\-list [ \-full ]
.br
.B busybox \-\-install
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-u
|
.IR dir ]
.br
.B busybox \-\-uninstall
.RB [ \-n ]
.I file
.br
.B busybox \-\-help
.RI [ applet ]
.br
.B busybox \-\-version
.br
.B busybox \-\-busybox
.I applet
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B busybox
is a single program,
.IR busybox.exe ,
that contains the shell and most of the Unix commands in w64devkit.
Each command it contains is called an applet.
An applet is a small implementation of the command of the same name,
usually with fewer options than its GNU or BSD namesake.
The kit's
.B busybox
is busybox-w32, the Windows port of BusyBox, built with w64devkit's own
patches and configuration; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
Many applets implement commands that POSIX specifies in its Shell and
Utilities volume.
The Open Group publishes the standard at
.IR https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/ .
.PP
With no arguments,
.B busybox
prints its version, a usage summary, and the list of applets on
standard output, and exits.
.PP
When its first argument is
.BR \-\-help ,
every applet except
.BR [ ,
.BR echo ,
.BR false ,
.BR test ,
and
.B true
prints its usage on standard output and exits with status 0, ignoring
any further arguments.
In the shell, a builtin of the same name, such as
.B printf
or
.BR kill ,
runs instead and treats
.B \-\-help
as an ordinary argument.
.SS busybox in w64devkit
w64devkit is a portable development kit for C and C++ on Windows.
Its
.I bin
directory holds the compilers and other tools, and
.B busybox
provides the shell and the Unix commands beside them.
.PP
.I w64devkit.exe
puts
.I bin
at the front of
.B PATH
and starts
.B busybox
as a login shell,
.BR "sh \-l" ;
see
.BR w64devkit (1).
For most applets, the kit's
.I bin
directory also holds a launcher, so that programs other than
.BR busybox ,
such as
.B make
or
.BR cmd.exe ,
find
.B sh
and the applets through
.B PATH
like any other program; see
.BR Launchers .
.PP
Where the kit includes a fuller program, such as GNU make or Vim,
.B busybox
is built without the applet of that name; see
.BR "Applets left out" .
Some applets in turn run other programs of the kit:
.B man
formats pages with
.BR nroff ,
and automatic decompression runs
.B gunzip
and
.BR unzstd .
.SS Choosing the applet
.B busybox
runs the applet named by its first command-line word, the name it was
started under.
It converts this word to lowercase, and removes a leading
.BR \- ,
a final period, and a suffix of
.BR .exe ,
.BR .com ,
.BR .bat ,
.BR .cmd ,
or
.BR .sh .
The last path component of the result is the applet name, so that
.B LS.EXE
and
.B C:\ew64devkit\ebin\els.exe
both run
.BR ls .
.PP
When this name begins with
.BR busybox ,
as
.B busybox.exe
and
.B busybox64.exe
do,
.B busybox
runs as itself: it performs one of the
.B OPTIONS
or runs the applet named by its first argument.
It ignores a directory part in that argument, but the rest must be the
applet name exactly:
.B busybox ls
works, but
.B busybox LS
and
.B busybox ls.exe
do not.
.PP
For an unknown applet,
.B busybox
writes
.IB name ": applet not found"
to standard error and exits with status 127.
.SS Launchers
The kit's
.I bin
directory holds
.I busybox.exe
and, for most applets, a launcher named after the applet, such as
.IR ls.exe .
A launcher is a small separate program, not a link to
.IR busybox.exe .
It starts the
.I busybox.exe
in its own directory with its own command line unchanged, waits for
it, and exits with its exit status.
Since the command line begins with the launcher's name,
.B busybox
runs the applet of that name.
The launcher ignores Ctrl+C and leaves it to
.BR busybox ,
so that the launcher's caller does not see the command end while
.B busybox
is still running.
.PP
Launchers are a w64devkit addition.
On other systems,
.B busybox \-\-install
provides each applet as a link to the BusyBox program.
Hard links would require that step after the kit is unpacked, symbolic
links require administrator rights or developer mode, and copies of
.I busybox.exe
would take far more space than the launchers, which are about 3 kB
each.
A batch file is no substitute, since
.B cmd.exe
would run between the caller and
.B busybox
and ask
.B "Terminate batch job (Y/N)?"
on Ctrl+C.
.PP
These applets have no launcher:
.BR [ ,
.BR cdrop ,
.BR drop ,
.BR flock ,
.BR pdrop ,
.BR sha384sum ,
and
.BR stty .
Run them as
.B busybox
.IR applet ,
or by name in the kit's shell, which runs applets without searching
.BR PATH .
.SS Applets
This build has these applets.
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
and
.B lash
are names of the same shell.
