.SS Applets left out
The kit builds
.B busybox
without the following applets, and ships other programs under their
names:
.TP
.BR gzip ", " gunzip ", " zcat ", " unzip
tugz, which also provides
.B zip
and
.BR zipinfo ;
see
.BR gzip (1)
and
.BR unzip (1).
.TP
.B vi
Vim; see
.BR vim (1).
.TP
.B xxd
rexxd; see
.BR xxd (1).
.TP
.B uuidgen
The kit's own; see
.BR uuidgen (1).
.TP
.B make
GNU make; see
.BR make (1).
.TP
.BR ar ", " strings
GNU binutils.
.PP
The kit also leaves out these applets, with no replacement:
.BR ascii ,
.BR crond ,
.BR crontab ,
.BR dpkg ,
.BR dpkg\-deb ,
.BR ftpget ,
.BR ftpput ,
.BR link ,
.BR pdpmake ,
.BR rpm ,
.BR rpm2cpio ,
.BR tsort ,
and
.BR unlink .
.PP
There is no
.B [[
applet, and the shell has no
.B [[
command; use
.B test
or
.B [
instead, as described in
.BR test (1).
.PP
busybox-w32 also offers the shell under the name
.BR bash ,
which adds no bash features.
The kit leaves that name out, so that the shell never takes the place
of a real bash in
.BR PATH ;
run the shell as
.BR sh .
.SS Running commands
When the shell, or an applet such as
.BR env ,
.BR find ,
.BR time ,
.BR timeout ,
or
.BR xargs ,
runs a command, it finds the command as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
A command with the name of an applet runs that applet, even when
.B PATH
has a program of the same name, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
says otherwise.
.IP \(bu 2
A command given as
.IR /bin/name ,
.IR /usr/bin/name ,
.IR /sbin/name ,
or
.I /usr/sbin/name
runs the applet
.I name
when no such file exists.
.IP \(bu 2
Any other command without a directory part is looked up in the
directories in
.BR PATH ,
in order.
In each directory, the name is tried as given and then with
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
appended, unless it already ends in one of these or in a period.
An empty entry in
.B PATH
stands for the current directory.
.IP \(bu 2
A file that begins with
.B #!\&
runs under the interpreter that this line names.
For an interpreter in
.IR /bin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
or
.IR /usr/sbin ,
busybox runs the applet of that name if there is one, or else that file
if it exists, or else the program of that name in
.BR PATH ,
so that
.B #!/bin/sh
and
.B #!/usr/bin/env
lines work.
When the shell runs the file, this search uses
.B PATH
as it was when the shell started, ignoring changes made in the shell.
A file whose name ends in
.B .sh
and that has no
.B #!\&
line runs under
.BR sh .
.PP
busybox starts from a native Windows environment when its environment
has not passed through the busybox shell, as when
.B cmd.exe
or Explorer starts it.
It recognizes such an environment by the mixed-case names that Windows
gives
.BR Path ,
.BR ComSpec ,
and
.BR ProgramData ,
since the shell passes every variable on with its name in capitals.
When busybox starts from a native Windows environment, it removes a
single trailing semicolon from
.BR PATH .
.SH OPTIONS
.B busybox
recognizes these options only as its first argument.
Except for
.BR \-\-busybox ,
they apply only when
.B busybox
is started under its own name; under any other name, the first argument
belongs to the applet.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the name of each applet on its own line, in sorted order.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-full
Like
.BR \-\-list ,
but precede each name with two fields: an 8-column field that holds
.BR NOFORK ,
.BR noexec ,
or nothing, and the applet's conventional Unix directory, such as
.BR usr/bin/ .
The shell runs
.B NOFORK
applets within its own process.
Any longer argument that begins with
.B \-\-list
has the same effect.
.TP
\fB\-\-install\fR [\fB\-s\fR] [\fB\-u\fR | \fIdir\fR]
Create a hard link to
.I busybox.exe
for each applet, named after the applet with
.B .exe
appended.
The links go in
.IR dir ,
which is created if needed, or else in the directory that contains
.IR busybox.exe .
Existing files are left alone.
Other failures are reported, but do not change the exit status.
.RS
.TP
.B \-s
Create symbolic links instead.
Windows allows this only with administrator rights or in developer
mode.
.TP
.B \-u
Install into the directories
.IR /bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
and
.I /usr/sbin
of the system drive, creating them as needed, and put each applet in the
directory that
.B \-\-list\-full
shows for it.
.RE
.IP
The kit's launchers already provide most applets as commands; see
.BR Launchers .
Without
.B \-u
or
.IR dir ,
.B \-\-install
therefore adds links only for the applets that have no launcher.
.TP
\fB\-\-uninstall\fR [\fB\-n\fR] \fIfile\fR
Delete every hard link to
.IR file ,
including
.I file
itself.
Given
.IR busybox.exe ,
or any link that
.B \-\-install
made, this deletes
.I busybox.exe
and all its links, except that Windows refuses to delete the last link
to a running program.
The kit's launchers are not links, so given a launcher, this deletes
only that launcher.
Failures are reported, but do not change the exit status.
On Windows XP, which lacks the API for finding links, it does nothing.
.RS
.TP
.B \-n
Print the full path of each link instead of deleting it.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR [\fIapplet\fR]
Print the usage of
.I applet
on standard output and exit with status 0.
For an unknown
.IR applet ,
report it as described under
.B Choosing the applet
and exit with status 127.
Without
.IR applet ,
or when it is
.BR busybox ,
print what
.B busybox
prints with no arguments.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version on standard output and exit.
If other arguments follow,
.B busybox
takes
.B \-\-version
as an applet name, and fails.
.TP
\fB\-\-busybox\fR \fIapplet\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Run
.I applet
whatever name
.B busybox
was started under, even one that does not begin with
.BR busybox .
The rules of
.B Choosing the applet
apply to
.I applet
as if
.B busybox
had been started under that name:
.B "\-\-busybox LS.EXE"
runs
.BR ls .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The variables whose names begin with
.B BB_
are added by busybox-w32; upstream BusyBox has none of them.
busybox reads them from its environment, when it starts or when an
applet runs, so a variable set in the shell takes effect only once it is
exported.
The shell applies
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_SYSTEMROOT ,
and
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
to itself as soon as they are exported; see
.BR sh (1).
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialogs, such as for a drive with no
disk in it.
Otherwise busybox suppresses them, and the operation fails with an
error instead.
.TP
.B BB_FIX_BACKSLASH
When it is
.BR 1 ,
.B ls
converts backslashes in its operands to forward slashes, so that the
names it prints have forward slashes.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands wildcards in the command-line arguments of
.B busybox
when it starts.
This serves commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe ,
which does not expand wildcards itself.
The runtime expands quoted arguments too, and leaves a pattern that
matches nothing unchanged.
When the variable is unset, busybox then sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so that the busybox programs it starts, such as those that the shell
runs, do not expand their arguments a second time.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
The applets that the shell and other applets do not run by name, so that
a program in
.B PATH
runs instead.
It does not affect starting an applet with its launcher or with
.BR busybox .
The value is one of:
.RS
.TP
.B \-
Run no applet by name.
.TP
.B +
Run a program of the same name in
.BR PATH ,
when there is one, instead of any applet.
.TP
.I list
Applet names separated by spaces, commas, or semicolons.
The applets named before the first semicolon are never run by name, and
those named after it are replaced only when a program of that name is in
.BR PATH .
.RE
.IP
In the kit, the program found is usually the applet's own launcher in
.IR bin ,
which runs the applet anyway, unless a directory earlier in
.B PATH
has a program of that name; see
.BR Launchers .
.TP
.B BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
The older form of
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
used only when that is unset:
.B 1
means mode 1,
.B 2
means mode 5, and any other value means mode 0.
.TP
.B BB_SYSTEMROOT
A drive, such as
.BR D: ,
to use in place of the system drive, which is the drive that holds the
Windows system directory.
A directory works too.
The system drive is where
.B "\-\-install \-u"
installs, where the login shell looks for
.IR /etc/profile ,
and where
.B man
looks for its configuration files in
.IR /etc .
.TP
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
How busybox uses the console, as a number from 0 to 5.
In virtual terminal (VT) output mode, the console interprets the escape
sequences that applets write, such as for color.
Otherwise busybox interprets the common ones itself, through the console
API.
In VT input mode, the console sends escape sequences for keys.
.RS
.TP
.B 0
Use neither VT output nor VT input.
.TP
.B 1
Force VT output.
.TP
.B 2
Force VT input.
.TP
.B 3
Force VT output and VT input.
.TP
.B 4
Use VT output and VT input only when the console already has them
enabled.
.TP
.B 5
Like 4, but also enable VT output when the console supports it.
.RE
.IP
The default is 5, or 0 when
.B CONEMUPID
is set or under Wine.
A number outside this range means 5, and a value that is not a number
means 0.
Modes 0 to 3 are meant for testing.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal, applied before each applet runs.
The default is
.BR 002 .
The owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
makes new files read-only.
The group and other write bits,
.BR 022 ,
only remove those bits from the permissions that busybox reports.
The shell's
.B umask
command sets and exports this variable.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
The terminal width and height for applets that fit their output to the
terminal, such as
.B ls
and
.BR less ,
and the width of the applet list that
.B busybox
prints with no arguments.
By default they are the size of the console window, or 80 by 24 when
neither standard output nor standard error is a console.
When set, they override the console window size.
A value of 1 or less, or of 30000 or more, means 80 for
.B COLUMNS
and 24 for
.BR LINES .
.TP
.B HOME
When it is unset, busybox uses the user profile, the folder that Windows
keeps for the account running the program, such as
.IR C:/Users/name .
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set determines how text is handled.
When its value is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes, and console output is written without code page
conversion.
Otherwise text is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, and single
bytes when it is not.
.TP
.B PATH
The directories searched for external programs; see
.BR "Running commands" .
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The directory for temporary files.
When it is unset, busybox uses
.BR TMP ,
then
.BR TEMP .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, for
.B busybox
with no arguments or with one of the
.BR OPTIONS .
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments to
.B \-\-install
or
.BR \-\-uninstall .
.TP
.B 127
The applet was not found.
.TP
.B 382
A launcher could not start
.IR busybox.exe ,
such as when it is missing from the launcher's directory.
The launcher writes a message that begins with
.B w64devkit:
to standard error.
The kit's shell sees this status as 126, its low byte.
.PP
Otherwise the exit status is that of the applet.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the applets:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox \-\-list
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the usage of an applet:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox \-\-help sort
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run an applet that has no launcher:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox sha384sum kit.zip
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the applets that the shell runs in its own process:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox \-\-list\-full | grep NOFORK
.EE
.RE
.PP
In the shell, run
.I wget.exe
from
.I ~/bin
instead of the
.B wget
applet:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ export PATH="$HOME/bin;$PATH"
$ export BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS=wget
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Paths may use forward or back slashes.
Prefer forward slashes, since in the shell a backslash quotes the next
character.
An absolute path has a drive, as in
.IR c:/path ,
or is a UNC path, as in
.IR //host/share/path .
A path such as
.I /path
is relative to the root of the current drive.
.PP
A path such as
.I c:name
is relative to the current directory of drive
.IR c: ,
as in Windows, and
.I c:/
is the root of the drive.
A bare drive such as
.I c:
is that current directory: applets list and recurse into it, and name
its entries
.IR c:name .
This is a w64devkit change; see below.
.PP
busybox maps these Unix device names wherever it opens or examines a
file, including in shell redirections:
.TP
.I /dev/null
The Windows device
.BR NUL .
.TP
.I /dev/tty
The console, the Windows device
.BR CON .
.TP
.IR /dev/stdin ", " /dev/stdout ", " /dev/stderr
File descriptors 0, 1, and 2.
.TP
.I /dev/fd/N
File descriptor
.IR N .
.TP
.IR /dev/zero ", " /dev/urandom
Built-in sources of zero bytes and of random bytes, which discard
writes.
.PP
A device name must be spelled exactly as shown, and
.I C:/dev/null
is an ordinary path.
Programs other than busybox see these names as ordinary paths, so give
them
.B NUL
instead.
A shell redirection such as
.B >/dev/null
works for any program, since the shell opens the file.
The Windows names
.B NUL
and
.B CON
work wherever a file is opened, in busybox as in any program, but
.B test \-e
and similar checks do not find them.
.PP
Files and standard streams are binary, with no CRLF translation:
applets write LF line endings.
.PP
Owners and permissions are synthesized.
A file shows as writable unless it is read-only.
It shows as executable when its name ends in
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.BR .cmd ,
or when its contents begin with
.B #!\&
or are a Windows executable, that is, a PE program that is not a DLL.
busybox reads the contents only of files whose names do not end in
.BR .dll ,
and only when the data is stored locally rather than in the cloud.
.PP
busybox has a manifest that makes its ANSI code page UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 or later, so that file names, arguments, and text may
contain any Unicode character.
On older systems they are limited to the system's ANSI code page.
Console output is converted to the console's code page, unless
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
or
.B LANG
is
.BR C .
If both of the console's code pages are 850 when busybox first uses the
console, it changes them to 858, which adds the euro sign.
.PP
w64devkit makes these changes to busybox-w32:
.IP \(bu 2
The login shell reads the kit's
.IR etc/profile ,
in the directory above
.IR bin ,
after
.I /etc/profile
on the system drive.
busybox-w32 looks for
.I etc/profile
in the directory that holds
.I busybox.exe
instead; see
.BR w64devkit (1).
.IP \(bu 2
Line editing in the shell never beeps.
.IP \(bu 2
In the shell, Tab does nothing where a command name should be typed but
none has been.
busybox-w32 offers every applet, builtin, and program in
.B PATH
there, which takes seconds on Windows.
.IP \(bu 2
The shell keeps 1024 lines of history by default, and
.B HISTSIZE
may raise this to 16384.
busybox-w32 keeps 384 lines, and at most 1023.
.IP \(bu 2
The shell's
.B noiconify
option is on by default, so that
.B noconsole
hides the console window instead of minimizing it; see
.BR sh (1).
.IP \(bu 2
The shell has no
.B [[
command.
.IP \(bu 2
Automatic decompression, as in
.B tar
and
.BR man ,
recognizes zstd data and decompresses it by running the kit's
.BR unzstd ,
and
.B tar
has a
.B \-\-zstd
option.
.IP \(bu 2
Automatic decompression of gzip data runs the kit's
.B gunzip
instead of built-in code.
.IP \(bu 2
.B man
finds the kit's pages and formats them with the kit's
.BR nroff ;
see
.BR man (1).
.IP \(bu 2
.B less \-R
passes color and font escape sequences through; see
.BR less (1).
.IP \(bu 2
A bare drive such as
.I c:
means the drive's current directory everywhere.
In busybox-w32 it means that directory only when an applet examines the
file itself.
Applets that list or recurse into a bare drive start at the drive's root
instead, so that
.B "rm \-rf c:"
removes files from the root.
.IP \(bu 2
As it does for
.B .\&
and
.BR .. ,
.B rm
refuses to remove a bare drive.
.IP \(bu 2
The 64-bit
.B busybox
also runs on Windows older than 10 version 1903, with the system's
ANSI code page.
The 64-bit Unicode build of busybox-w32 refuses to run there.
.IP \(bu 2
Some applets are left out, and the shell is not available as
.BR bash ;
see
.BR "Applets left out" .
.IP \(bu 2
Most applets have a launcher in
.IR bin ;
see
.BR Launchers .
